X('assessable-value-cif-landing-charges',[
 ('A worked example',['A buyer in Delhi imports goods with an invoice value of US$ 20,000 on an FOB basis. Freight is US$ 1,500 and insurance is US$ 100. The assessable value in dollars is US$ 21,600, which is then converted to rupees at the customs exchange rate notified for the week of filing. If the rate is, say, 85, the assessable value is about ₹18,36,000. Duty and IGST are worked out on that figure, not on the invoice value alone.','If freight and insurance are not known, customs rules allow a standard addition to the FOB value. That is why it is worth keeping the freight invoice and the insurance certificate: the actual figures can be lower than the standard addition.']),
 ('What gets added',['Freight and insurance to the Indian port or airport, if the invoice is FOB or EXW.','Commissions, brokerage and packing costs that the buyer pays as part of the sale.','Royalties, licence fees and assists where they are connected with the sale. See our guide on <a href="guides/royalty-licence-fees-assists-customs-value" style="color:#0A6CC2">royalty, licence fees and assists</a>.']),
 ('Mistakes to avoid',['Declaring only the goods value when the invoice is FOB. The department will add freight, and your bill will be re-assessed.','Using the bank rate instead of the customs rate. The customs rate is notified separately.','Splitting an invoice to show a lower price on paper. That is undervaluation, and it carries penalties.'])],
 [('Is the assessable value the same as the invoice value?','Not always. It starts with the transaction value and then adds freight, insurance and other costs under the valuation rules, and it is converted to rupees at the customs rate.')])
X('customs-valuation-methods-india',[
 ('The methods in plain words',['Method one is the transaction value: the price actually paid, adjusted for the additions in the rules. It is used first, and in most cases it is the only one needed.','If it cannot be used, Customs moves down the ladder: the value of identical goods, then similar goods, then a deductive value worked back from the resale price in India, then a computed value from cost of production, and finally a fall-back method based on reasonable means. The order of the last two can be switched at the importer\'s request, under the rules.']),
 ('Why a price gets rejected',['The price looks far below that of the same goods at the same time, with no explanation.','The buyer and seller are related, and the relationship has influenced the price.','The invoice does not match the contract, the bank payment or the supplier\'s catalogue.','Some of the price is paid outside the invoice, in a separate agreement.']),
 ('Building your file',['Keep the contract, purchase order, proforma invoice, bank remittance advice and any price list for each shipment. If the price is low for a good reason, such as a bulk discount, a seasonal sale or an old stock clearance, write that reason down at the time. A clear paper trail is the best defence if the officer asks questions months later.'])],
 [('Can Customs reject my invoice price?','Yes, if it has reasonable doubt about the declared value, and it must then give reasons and work out the value under the other methods in the rules. You can ask for a written reason and challenge it.')])
X('royalty-licence-fees-assists-customs-value',[
 ('Reading the rule',['Royalties and licence fees are added to the customs value when they relate to the goods being imported and are paid as a condition of sale. A fee for the right to use a trademark on the imported goods is a typical case. A payment for the right to manufacture the goods in India after import is usually not, but the facts matter.','The importer is expected to declare these payments at the time of filing, even if they are paid to a third party or later in time.']),
 ('Assists in practice',['An assist is something the buyer supplies free or at a reduced cost for the making of the goods: tooling, moulds, designs, engineering work done outside India, or materials. If the buyer sends a mould to the supplier in China to make parts for India, the cost of the mould is added to the value of the parts, spread over the production in a reasonable way.','This catches many importers by surprise because no money changes hands on the invoice.']),
 ('How to prepare',['List every payment to the supplier or a related party, even if it is not on the invoice.','Ask your broker whether the amount is dutiable before you sign the licence or supply agreement, not after.','Keep a record of the way you spread the cost of assists over the shipments.'])],
 [('Do I pay duty on royalty paid to the supplier?','If the royalty is related to the imported goods and is a condition of the sale, it is added to the customs value. The treatment depends on the agreement, so get advice before you shape it.')])
X('social-welfare-surcharge-explained',[
 ('The arithmetic',['The surcharge is a percentage of the basic customs duty, normally ten per cent. If the basic duty on a shipment is ₹1,00,000, the surcharge is ₹10,000. It is not charged on the goods value directly. Because IGST is charged on the total of value, duty and surcharge, the surcharge also increases the IGST slightly.','On goods with zero basic duty, the surcharge is zero too, which is why many electronic components show only IGST in the calculation.']),
 ('When it does not apply',['Where a notification exempts the goods from the surcharge, or the basic duty is exempted in full, no surcharge is payable.','Some duties, such as certain additional or special duties, are not subject to the surcharge. The calculation on the bill of entry shows each head separately.']),
 ('Why it matters to a quote',['Importers sometimes work out landed cost with the basic duty and the IGST and forget the surcharge. On large shipments the difference is real money. Use the <a href="customs-duty-calculator" style="color:#0A6CC2">duty calculator</a> to see every head on one screen, and remember that rates change by notification and the Budget.'])],
 [('Is the surcharge refundable?','Not in general. It is part of the duty cost. Where the basic duty is refunded or reduced after a re-assessment or an appeal, the surcharge is adjusted with it.')])
X('igst-on-imports-and-input-tax-credit',[
 ('A numerical example',['An importer pays ₹2,00,000 as IGST on a bill of entry. In the same month the importer sells the goods and charges ₹3,00,000 of GST to customers. The importer can use the ₹2,00,000 as credit and pay only the balance of ₹1,00,000 in cash, if the conditions for credit are met. The credit appears in the electronic credit ledger once the bill of entry is matched with the GST system.','This is why IGST on imports is a cash-flow cost more than a final cost for a registered business that sells taxable goods.']),
 ('Conditions for the credit',['The bill of entry must show the importer\'s GST number, and the details must match the data the customs department sends to the GST system.','The goods must be used for business and not be on a list where credit is blocked.','The IGST must actually be paid. Credit is not available on duty that is still under dispute and unpaid.']),
 ('Common errors',['Filing the bill with the wrong GST number, so the credit appears in someone else\'s account or not at all.','Missing the credit because an amendment to the bill was not reflected in the GST data.','Assuming that basic customs duty is also creditable. It is not.'])],
 [('Can an unregistered person claim IGST paid on imports?','No. Only a registered person who uses the goods for business can claim the credit.')])
X('aidc-agriculture-infrastructure-development-cess',[
 ('Where you meet it',['The cess was introduced in the 2021 Budget on a list of goods, at a rate that differs by product. For many of these goods, the basic customs duty was reduced at the same time, so the total burden stayed roughly the same. The change was made to fund agricultural infrastructure.','It appears on the bill of entry as its own line, separate from the basic duty and the surcharge.']),
 ('How it affects the total',['The cess is added to the duty before IGST is worked out for the goods it applies to, so it can change the IGST amount. When you read a duty line, check the AIDC column as well as the basic duty.','In our data the line for non-monetary gold and silver shows an AIDC of five per cent beside a ten per cent basic duty, which is one example of a product where the cess matters.']),
 ('Staying current',['The goods covered and the rates are set by notification and the Finance Act, and they change. Do not carry a cess rate from one year to the next. Check the current notification or ask your broker, especially for metals, edible oils, fuels and alcohol, where the changes have been most frequent.'])],
 [('Is AIDC an extra tax on top of the basic duty?','It is a separate cess on the listed goods. In several cases the basic duty was reduced when it was introduced, so the total is not always higher.')])
X('anti-dumping-duty-explained',[
 ('How a case runs',['The domestic industry files a complaint with the authority that examines trade remedies. After an investigation, the authority recommends a duty, and the finance ministry decides whether to impose it. The notice names the product, the exporting countries and often the exporters, with a duty for each.','The rate can differ a lot between a named exporter and "all others", so the supplier you buy from can matter.']),
 ('What it means for an importer',['The duty is paid on top of the other duties, and it is not creditable like IGST. It can turn a profitable import into a loss.','A supplier\'s name and the country of origin on the bill of entry decide which rate applies. A wrong declaration can bring a penalty.','The duty may be for a limited period, often five years, and can be reviewed or extended.']),
 ('Check before you order',['Search the product description and the HS code in the current notifications, and ask your broker to confirm that no duty is in force on your product and supplier. Do this before you place the order, because the duty applies at the date of import, not the date of the order. The calculator shows a note when a duty line has anti-dumping conditions, but the notification is what counts.'])],
 [('Does anti-dumping duty apply to every supplier?','No. The notification lists the countries and sometimes the producers. A named exporter may have its own rate, and others fall under a residual rate or no duty.')])
X('safeguard-and-countervailing-duty',[
 ('How they differ in plain words',['Anti-dumping duty responds to a foreign exporter selling at a price below its normal value. Countervailing duty responds to a foreign government subsidy that makes the goods cheaper. Safeguard duty responds to a sudden surge of imports that harms the domestic industry, whatever the price or the cause.','Safeguard duty usually applies to imports from many countries, with limited exceptions, while the other two are tied to named countries or exporters.']),
 ('Time limits',['Safeguard measures are temporary by design and are reduced in steps over their life. Anti-dumping and countervailing duties usually last up to five years, unless extended after a review. Always check the end date, because the duty may expire or be extended close to your shipment date.']),
 ('What to do as an importer',['Ask your supplier about the status of the product and any duty already in force.','Keep the supplier invoice, the country of origin proof and the production details, because they decide which rate applies.','Budget for a duty that can appear or end in the middle of a contract, and write the risk into the price terms.'])],
 [('Are these duties part of IGST credit?','No. They are separate trade remedy duties and are a cost. IGST on the total can still be creditable for a registered business.')])
X('fta-preferential-duty-caro-tar',[
 ('The steps of a claim',['Check that the product is covered by the agreement and find the preferential rate in the schedule. Get the certificate of origin from the exporter\'s country in the form the agreement requires. Declare the claim on the bill of entry, with the notification and serial number. Keep the supplier\'s cost sheet and the production details, because the department may verify the origin.','The preferential rate applies only to goods that meet the rules of origin, which usually need real processing in the partner country, not just packing.']),
 ('CAROTAR and its purpose',['The rules on origin verification set out what an importer must keep and give to Customs when a preference is claimed. Importers are expected to exercise due care, and officers can ask for supporting records and, in some cases, a verification from the exporting country\'s authority. A claim can be denied if the importer cannot produce the documents.','Keep origin papers for the period the rules say, and be ready to produce them months after the clearance.']),
 ('A typical case',['An importer buys from a supplier in a partner country and claims zero basic duty. If the certificate is in the wrong format or the goods were made in a third country, the claim fails and the full duty is demanded, with interest and a possible penalty. A short check of the origin rule before you order is much cheaper than a dispute later.'])],
 [('Does a trade agreement remove IGST as well?','No. Agreements reduce or remove the basic customs duty on qualifying goods. IGST and other taxes still apply.')])
X('customs-exchange-rate-cbic-notified',[
 ('How the date is chosen',['CBIC notifies the exchange rate for imports and exports at regular intervals, and it can be revised in between when currencies move a lot. The rate in force on the date the bill of entry or shipping bill is presented is the one used, under the rules. The rate for imports differs from the rate for exports, because the two rates are set separately and are not the same number.','This is why the rate in your bank statement is not the rate Customs uses.']),
 ('A small example',['An importer files on a day when the notified import rate for the dollar is 85. A shipment with an assessable value of US$ 10,000 gets a value of ₹8,50,000. If the same bill had been filed after a new notification set the rate at 86, the value would be ₹8,60,000. The difference of ₹10,000 in value changes the duty by a few thousand rupees.']),
 ('How to use this',['Check the current notification before you work out landed cost for a quote. For big shipments, ask your broker which rate will apply on the expected filing date. A bill filed a few days earlier or later can fall under a different notification.'])],
 [('Where do I find the customs exchange rate?','CBIC publishes it as a notification on its website and the ICEGATE portal. Your broker will use the rate in force on the date of filing.')])
X('hs-code-vs-hsn-vs-itc-hs',[
 ('How the digits build up',['The first two digits give the chapter, the next two the heading, and the next two the sub-heading. Together these six digits are the same in most countries that use the Harmonized System. India extends this to eight digits in the Customs Tariff, which fixes the duty. The ITC-HS schedule uses the same eight digits for import and export policy.','GST uses the HSN, which is six or eight digits depending on the taxpayer\'s turnover, and it is based on the same system.']),
 ('Which one to use where',['Customs duty: the eight-digit tariff code on the bill of entry.','Import and export policy: the ITC-HS code in the DGFT schedule.','GST on domestic sales and the invoice: the HSN code.','Foreign customs: the buyer\'s country will use at least the first six digits and add its own extension.']),
 ('A caution on classification',['The code is chosen by what the goods are, not by what they are called in the trade. Two products with the same name can fall under different codes, and the duty can differ widely. If your code is uncertain, ask for the classification in writing from your broker, or consider an advance ruling for a regular import.'])],
 [('Are the HS code and the HSN code the same?','The first six digits are the same system. HSN is the name used in GST in India, while HS code is the international name. Customs in India uses the eight-digit tariff code.')])
X('advance-ruling-customs-india',[
 ('What a ruling covers',['An applicant can ask the authority for a ruling on questions such as the classification of goods, the applicability of a notification, or the principles of valuation for a proposed import or export. The ruling is given in writing after the authority hears the applicant, and it is meant to give certainty before the goods move.','The question must be about a transaction that is proposed or under way, not one that is already the subject of a dispute.']),
 ('The process in outline',['The application is made on the prescribed form with a fee, and it needs a description of the goods, the facts and your own view of the answer.','The authority may ask for more information and hold a hearing.','The ruling is usually delivered within a set time, and a copy is given to the applicant and the department.']),
 ('Is it worth it?',['For a one-off shipment, the time can be longer than the delay it saves. For a product you will import for years, such as a new type of machine or a product with an unclear code, a ruling can prevent repeated disputes. It binds the applicant and the department for the same goods, as long as the facts and law do not change.'])],
 [('Is an advance ruling binding?','Yes, on the applicant and the department for the goods and facts described, unless the law or the facts change or the ruling was obtained by misrepresentation.')])
X('customs-demand-notice-appeal-and-refund',[
 ('Steps after a notice arrives',['Read the notice for the date, the amount and the reason, and note the last day to reply. Do not ignore it, because an order can be passed against you without your reply. Gather the bill of entry, invoice, contracts and any correspondence for the shipment concerned.','Ask your broker or an adviser to prepare the reply. A short, factual reply with documents is better than a long one with arguments and no proof.']),
 ('Time limits to remember',['The reply to a show cause notice must be filed within the time the notice gives. Ask for an extension in writing if you need one.','An appeal against an order goes to the Commissioner (Appeals) within the statutory time from the date of the order. A further appeal can go to the tribunal.','A refund claim has its own time limit, usually counted from the date duty was paid, so file promptly.']),
 ('Paying under protest',['If the amount is small, many importers pay under protest to release goods and preserve the right to claim a refund after the dispute is decided. If it is large, a pre-deposit may be needed to file an appeal. Take advice on which path is cheaper before you decide.'])],
 [('What is a show cause notice?','It is a written notice asking you to explain why the department should not demand duty, interest or a penalty. It gives you a chance to reply with facts and documents before an order is passed.')])
